{{#extend "layout" showSource=true}}

  {{#content "head"}}
  {{!-- The stylesheet the pipeline step wrote, linked like any other asset:
        `asset` resolves it out of the manifest the copy filled in, so the
        template never has to know a tool produced it. --}}
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{root}}{{asset 'css/generated.css'}}">
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "main"}}
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Example 10 &middot; asset pipeline</p>
    <h1>A tool kiss knows nothing about</h1>
    <p>
      The colours on this page were not written by hand and were not compiled by kiss.
      A command ran before the asset copy, wrote a stylesheet into the assets folder, and
      the copy picked it up like any other file.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="token-card">
    <p>
      <span class="token-badge">generated</span>
      This card is styled by <code>css/generated.css</code>, built from
      <code>styles/tokens.json</code> by <code>{{model.command}}</code>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="card">
    <h2>One helper, four kinds of reference</h2>
    <p>
      Every reference below is emitted by the same <code>\{{asset}}</code> call. Two of them
      name a file this build wrote and are checked against it; two carry their own origin and
      are handed straight back.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <svg width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><use href="{{root}}{{asset 'img/icons.svg#bean'}}"></use></svg>
        <code>\{{root}}\{{asset 'img/icons.svg#bean'}}</code> &mdash; a file the copy emitted,
        plus a sprite fragment. The <code>#&hellip;</code> comes off before the lookup and goes
        back on after it, so cache busting can rename the file and the fragment still names the
        right symbol. A path into the build, so it takes <code>\{{root}}</code> like any other.
        (An external sprite needs the page to be <em>served</em> &mdash; <code>--dev</code> does
        that; opened straight off the file system the browser refuses to fetch it.)
      </li>
      <li>
        <img src="{{asset model.badge}}" alt="" width="18" height="18">
        <code>\{{asset model.badge}}</code> &mdash; a <code>data:</code> URI, drawn by the
        browser from the string itself. It carries no <code>//</code>, which is exactly how it
        used to be mistaken for a path into the build and fail it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>\{{asset config.cdn.fonts}}</code> &mdash; renders
        <code>{{asset config.cdn.fonts}}</code>, unchanged and unlooked-up, with
        <strong>no <code>\{{root}}</code></strong>: a URL that names its own origin has no base
        to climb back to.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>\{{asset config.cdn.insights}}</code> &mdash; protocol-relative, no scheme at all:
        renders <code>{{asset config.cdn.insights}}</code>, so a snippet pasted from a vendor
        works as given. Prefer <code>https://</code> when the choice is yours; this form is here
        because plenty of CDN snippets still hand it to you.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <div class="card">
    {{> "newsletter"}}
  </div>
  {{/content}}

  {{#content "demo"}}
  <p>
    <code>config.assets.pipeline</code> is an ordered list of
    <code>{ name?, run, watch?, cwd? }</code> steps. Every <code>run</code> is executed
    through a shell, in order, awaited, <strong>before</strong> the asset copy &mdash; which is
    what puts the generated stylesheet in the build in time for the first page to link it.
    A step that exits non-zero fails the build like a failed page:
    <code>complete()</code> rejects and the failure is named
    <code>&lt;pipeline: tokens&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Run this with <code>--dev</code> and the step's <code>watch</code> command is started
    too &mdash; once, after <code>run</code> has succeeded &mdash; and kept for the session.
    Edit <code>styles/tokens.json</code> while it is up: the tool
    recompiles the stylesheet on its own, kiss's assets watcher copies it into the build,
    and the browser reloads. <code>close()</code> ends the watch process, so nothing is
    left running when the server stops.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each command inherits <code>process.env</code> plus <code>KISS_BUILD</code>,
    <code>KISS_ASSETS</code> and <code>KISS_DEV</code> &mdash; this example's tool writes to
    <code>KISS_ASSETS</code> rather than guessing where the site keeps its assets. In a real
    project the step is your actual toolchain: <code>npx @tailwindcss/cli -i
    src/styles/site.css -o src/assets/css/site.css --minify</code>, with the same command
    plus <code>--watch</code> as the <code>watch</code> half.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every URL these pages emit goes through <code>\{{asset}}</code>, which is the point of the
    helper rather than an accident of it: the references kiss owns are checked against the build
    &mdash; a path no copy emitted <strong>fails the build</strong> rather than shipping a 404 &mdash;
    and the rest are handed back untouched. Anything carrying a scheme (<code>https:</code>,
    <code>data:</code>, <code>mailto:</code>) or a protocol-relative <code>//host/&hellip;</code>
    names no file this build wrote, so there is nothing to look up and nothing to fail.
    A <code>?query</code> is split off like a <code>#fragment</code>:
    <code>\{{asset 'css/generated.css?v=2'}}</code> renders
    <code>{{asset 'css/generated.css?v=2'}}</code>, keeping your query and suppressing the one
    <code>assets.version</code> would have added &mdash; two query strings on one URL is not a
    thing, and yours is the explicit one.
  </p>
  {{/content}}

{{/extend}}
